@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Using CucumberSwift with Swift Package Manager") {
        Add CucumberSwift to a unit test target in an Xcode project with Swift Package Manager, write a first feature file, and let CucumberSwift suggest its step definitions.

        You end up with a setup like the [GettingStarted](https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwiftSample/tree/main/Tuist/GettingStarted) sample: a macOS unit test target, a `Features` folder, and step definitions that use Cucumber expressions. The sample writes them as functions. This tutorial writes them with the step definition macros, the recommended style, as the [StepDefinitionMacros](https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwiftSample/tree/main/Tuist/StepDefinitionMacros) sample does.
    }

    @Section(title: "Add CucumberSwift to your test target") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            You need Xcode 26.4 or later, and an Xcode project with a unit test target. The step definition macros need a package trait that earlier Xcode versions can't turn on in a project. For earlier versions, see <doc:Using-Step-Definition-Macros#Before-Xcode-264>. This tutorial uses a macOS unit test target, like the sample. The steps are the same for an iOS or tvOS app's unit test target.

            If your project has no unit test target yet, choose File → New → Target… and add a Unit Testing Bundle. Choose XCTest as its testing system.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Choose File → Add Package Dependencies…, and enter `https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwift` in the search field. Keep the dependency rule "Up to Next Major Version", starting at the latest release, and select Add Package.

                The sheet has no setting for package traits. So afterwards, turn on the package's `Macros` trait, add the swift-syntax package to the project as well, and add the `CucumberSwiftMacros` library to your unit test target, not to your app. <doc:Using-Step-Definition-Macros#Set-up-an-Xcode-project> gives each step.

                @Image(source: "SPM-Add-Package.png", alt: "Xcode's Add Package Dependencies sheet, with the CucumberSwift package found from its URL, the dependency rule Up to Next Major Version from 6.6.0 to 7.0.0, and the Calculator project selected.")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Add a Features folder") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            CucumberSwift looks for your `.feature` files in a folder named `Features`, inside the test bundle. The name is case sensitive. Xcode must copy the folder into the bundle as a folder, so add it as a folder reference.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In the Finder, create a folder named `Features` in your project's folder, next to the folder of your test target.

                > Important: Don't create it inside your test target's folder if that folder is a synchronized folder (the default for targets created in Xcode 16 and later). Xcode copies each file of a synchronized folder into the bundle on its own, without the `Features` folder, and CucumberSwift then finds no features.

                @Image(source: "CreateFeaturesFolder.png", alt: "The Finder, showing a new Features folder next to the test target's folder.")
            }

            @Step {
                Choose File → Add Files to "Calculator"… (the menu item has your project's name), select the `Features` folder, and select Add. In the options sheet, set Action to "Reference files in place" and Groups to "Create folders", and select your test target only.

                @Image(source: "AddFeaturesFolder.png", alt: "Xcode's options sheet for adding the Features folder: Action is Reference files in place, Groups is Create folders, and only the CalculatorTests target is selected.")
            }

            @Step {
                Xcode adds the folder as a synchronized folder. A synchronized folder copies each file in it into the bundle on its own. Select `Features` in the project navigator, choose View → Inspectors → File, and set Build Rules to "Apply Once to Folder". Xcode then copies the folder, with everything in it, into the test bundle.

                Changing the build rule removes the folder from your test target. Under Target Membership, select the add button (+), choose your test target, and select Save.

                @Image(source: "FeaturesFolderBuildRules.png", alt: "The File inspector for the Features folder: Build Rules is Apply Once to Folder, and Target Membership lists CalculatorTests.")
            }

            @Step {
                Add a feature file to the `Features` folder. You can group feature files in subfolders.

                > Tip: To have Xcode color the Gherkin in your `.feature` files, install CucumberSwift's syntax highlighting. See <doc:Running-Tests-In-Xcode#Highlight-feature-files>.

                @Code(name: "Calculator.feature", file: "Calculator.feature")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Write the step definitions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            CucumberSwift finds your step definitions through an extension of `Cucumber` that conforms to `StepImplementation`. When a step has no step definition, CucumberSwift writes one for you.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Replace the test file Xcode created in your test target with this one. `bundle` returns the bundle that holds the `Features` folder: your test bundle.

                The file imports `CucumberSwiftMacros`, and that module also imports CucumberSwift. The extension adds a conformance to a type and a protocol that both come from CucumberSwift, and `@retroactive` says that you mean it. Without it, Xcode warns about the conformance, in both the Swift 5 and Swift 6 language modes.

                @Code(name: "StepDefinitions.swift", file: "StepDefinitions-Macros-Empty.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Turn off parallel testing for your test target. XCTest's parallel workers don't run CucumberSwift's scenarios correctly. Each scenario can run once in every worker, or not at all, and the test run can still pass. Xcode turns parallel testing on for new test targets. To try parallel testing anyway, see <doc:Running-Scenarios-In-Parallel>.

                Choose Product → Test Plan → Edit Test Plan. Next to your test target, select Options…, and set Parallelization to Disabled. Leave "Automatically include new tests" on. CucumberSwift creates its tests while the tests run. With the option off, the test plan runs only the tests it already lists, so your scenarios don't run, and the run still succeeds. If Xcode says that the autocreated test plan is an in-memory document, choose Save so that the setting is kept.

                @Image(source: "TestPlanParallelization.png", alt: "The test plan editor with the Options popover for CalculatorTests: Automatically include new tests is on, and Parallelization is Disabled.")
            }

            @Step {
                Run the tests with ⌘U. The scenario fails at its first step, because no step definition matches it. The failure points at the step's line in the feature file, and its message contains a step definition for that step. The steps after it are skipped.

                @Image(source: "UndefinedStepFailure.png", alt: "The Test navigator after the first run: the first step of Add two numbers has failed and the other three are skipped. Calculator.feature is open at line 5, with the failure message and its suggested step definition, a `#Given` macro with an `Int` argument.")
            }

            @Step {
                The failure suggests a step definition for the first step only. CucumberSwift also writes one for every step without a step definition, including skipped steps, to a file. To open it, right-click the test `GenerateStepsStubsIfNecessary` in the Test navigator and choose Jump to Report. Under Pending Steps, open the file whose name starts with `GENERATED_Unimplemented_Step_Definitions`. Xcode adds a unique ID to the end of the file name.

                @Image(source: "ViewGeneratedStubs.png", alt: "The test report for GenerateStepsStubsIfNecessary, with the GENERATED_Unimplemented_Step_Definitions file under Pending Steps.")
            }

            @Step {
                Paste the suggested step definitions into `setupSteps()`. Each one is a step definition macro, `#Given`, `#When` or `#Then`, that matches its step with a Cucumber expression. `{int}` matches a whole number, and the closure receives it as an `Int` argument. CucumberSwift suggests macros because your test target links `CucumberSwiftMacros` (see <doc:Matching-Steps#Step-definition-macros>). Each step fails until you replace its `XCTFail` line with your test code.

                > Note: To have regular expression literals suggested instead, turn on `Cucumber.generateRegexLiterals`. See <doc:Matching-Steps#Step-definitions-for-undefined-steps>.

                @Code(name: "StepDefinitions.swift", file: "StepDefinitions-Macros-Generated.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Replace each `XCTFail` line with your test code, and add what each step does. Run the tests again. They pass. To convert definitions you already have, see <doc:Using-Step-Definition-Macros#Convert-existing-step-definitions>.

                See <doc:Matching-Steps> for every way to match a step.

                @Code(name: "StepDefinitions.swift", file: "StepDefinitions-Macros.swift")
            }

            Your test target now has a setup like the one in the [GettingStarted](https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwiftSample/tree/main/Tuist/GettingStarted) sample in [CucumberSwiftSample](https://github.com/cucumberswift/CucumberSwiftSample). That sample is a working, tested project that you can compare with yours. The [sample projects](https://cucumberswift.org/CucumberSwiftSample/documentation/cucumberswiftsample/) show more setups, such as how your scenarios read in Xcode's test navigator. Next, <doc:Running-Tests-In-Xcode> sets up the rest of Xcode for CucumberSwift, and its plugins. The plugins check feature files while you build, fix their keywords and convert step definitions to macros.

            For a test target in a `Package.swift` that you run with `swift test`, instead of an Xcode project, see <doc:Running-Tests-With-Swift-Package-Manager>.

            CucumberSwift also runs in a UI test target, with the same setup. Add CucumberSwift, the `Features` folder and the step definitions to the UI test target, and drive your app from the steps with `XCUIApplication`. To test UIKit screens from a unit test target instead, see <doc:CucumberSwift+UIUTest>.
        }
    }
}
